# ls - list drawers in columns, coloured by kind

## SYNOPSIS
    ls [-1aAdFhlrRSt] [path | pattern ...]

## DESCRIPTION
Shows what is in a drawer, in columns that fit the window, in natural
order (`file9` before `file10`), upper and lower case alike, drawers and
files mixed. With no path it lists the current drawer.

A drawer lists its contents, a pattern (`#?` or `\*`) the names it
matches, a file just itself. Files and pattern matches come first as one
list, then each drawer, under its name when there are several.

In a window the names are coloured by their kind: drawers, links,
programs, scripts, archives, pictures, sounds, sources, text, icons and
hidden files each have a colour. When the output goes to a file or a
pipe, ls writes one name a line and no colours, so other commands can
read it.

Icons (`.info`) and files with the h bit are left out unless **-a** is
given, or a pattern names them.

## OPTIONS
- **-1** one name a line.
- **-a** also show `.info` icons and files with the h (hidden) bit.
- **-A** the same as **-a**.
- **-d** a drawer itself, not what is in it.
- **-F** a mark after each name: `/` drawers, `\*` programs, `@` links.
- **-h** sizes in K, M and G, easy to read (with **-l**).
- **-l** long form: protection bits, size, date, time, name, and the
  filenote on a line below.
- **-r** reverse the order.
- **-R** also list every drawer inside, all the way down (32 levels at
  most; links are not followed).
- **-S** by size, the biggest first.
- **-t** by date, the newest first.

## EXAMPLES
    ls
    ls -la Work:
    ls -lt Work:Downloads
    ls -lhS SYS:Libs
    ls SYS:C/#?dir#?
    ls -1 RAM: | wc -l

## RETURN CODES
0 fine, 5 a path could not be read or a pattern matched nothing,
10 bad arguments, 20 stopped with Ctrl-C.

## NOTES
Options may also come after the names (`ls Work: -la`); a `--` makes
everything after it a name.

The protection bits in the long form are as List shows them:
`hsparwed`. A letter means the bit is set (h s p a) or the action is
allowed (r w e d); `-` means not.

The colours are in **ENV:FileColours** (else ENVARC:FileColours), which
ls writes with its defaults when it is missing. Each line is either
`KIND CTERM,PLAIN`, the colour numbers (SGR) for CTerm and for a plain
Shell window, or `.EXT KIND`, saying that names ending in .ext are of
that kind. The kinds: drawer link program script archive picture sound
source text icon hidden. A program is a name with no ending and the e
bit, a script has the s bit, hidden the h bit, an icon ends in .info.
Ahiru and Maki read the same file.

The width of the window comes from the variable COLUMNS (csh keeps it),
else the console is asked.

`ls ?` shows the usage line.

